+# admin-api
+
+The privileged backend for the Organizational Design Tool: sign-in role
+resolution and every Admin Panel write (create/update/delete an employee,
+grant or revoke Admin, activate or deactivate an account).
+
+It replaces the Google Apps Script Web App that used to live at
+`apps-script/Code.gs`. Azerconnect's corporate proxy blocks the
+`google-app-script-base` application, so `script.google.com` was unreachable
+for every user on the network — which broke sign-in itself, not just the
+Admin Panel, since role resolution went through it. This function answers on
+the same `*.supabase.co` origin the app already uses for Supabase Auth.
+
+The logic is a faithful port: same action names, same request and response
+shapes, same server-side checks. `js/backend.js` is its only caller in the
+app (plus the three standalone one-off tools, which call it directly).
+
+## Deploy
+
+Both paths need the Supabase project's owner/admin access. Nothing else has
+to be configured: Supabase injects `SUPABASE_URL` and
+`SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` into the function's environment automatically,
+which is why the service_role key appears nowhere in this repository.
+
+### Option A — Supabase CLI
+
+```bash
+npm install -g supabase          # or: brew install supabase/tap/supabase
+supabase login
+supabase link --project-ref mkgkbqjvywhiginhjyjn
+supabase functions deploy admin-api --no-verify-jwt
+```
+
+### Option B — Dashboard (no local tooling)
+
+Dashboard → **Edge Functions** → **Deploy a new function** → **Via editor**,
+name it exactly `admin-api`, paste the contents of `index.ts`, deploy, then
+turn **Verify JWT with legacy secret** OFF in the function's settings.
+
+### Why `--no-verify-jwt`
+
+The platform's JWT gate is deliberately not used, for two reasons:
+
+1. This function verifies the caller's token itself, against Supabase's own
+   `/auth/v1/user` endpoint, and re-checks `admin_permissions` server-side
+   for every admin action. That check is the real gate and it cannot be
+   skipped by anything a client sends.
+2. `bootstrapAdmin` must be callable with no session at all — there is no
+   Admin to authenticate as the first time it runs.
+
+Turning it off does not make the function open: an unauthenticated caller
+gets `{"authorized": false}` or `{"status": "error", "message":
+"Unauthorized."}` and nothing else.
+
+## Verify a deploy
+
+```bash
+curl -X POST https://mkgkbqjvywhiginhjyjn.supabase.co/functions/v1/admin-api \
+  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
+  -d '{"action":"resolveRole","access_token":"not-a-real-token"}'
+```
+
+Expected: `{"authorized":false}`. That single response proves the function is
+deployed, reachable from this network, answering without a platform JWT, and
+correctly refusing an invalid token.
+
+Then sign in through `index.html`. A real account should land on its own
+dashboard, and an account with Admin permission should be able to open
+`admin.html`.
+
+## Bootstrap (only when no Admin exists at all)
+
+`bootstrap-setup.html` seeds the first Admin. It is gated by a `BOOTSTRAP_KEY`
+secret you set yourself, and refuses to run once any `admin_permissions` row
+exists:
+
+```bash
+supabase secrets set BOOTSTRAP_KEY=<a secret you choose>
+# ... run bootstrap-setup.html ...
+supabase secrets unset BOOTSTRAP_KEY
+```
+
+Granting Admin to an account that already exists does not need this — use the
+Admin Panel's Grant Admin button, or one SQL statement:
+
+```sql
+insert into admin_permissions (auth_user_id, is_admin, granted_at)
+select id, true, now() from auth.users where email = 'someone@azerconnect.az'
+on conflict (auth_user_id) do update set is_admin = true, granted_at = now();
+```
